ith literal 5"}]}, :event/kind :mutation, :event/at-ms 324249} {:event/kind :triage, :role :coder, :attempt 2, :decision "Gates green and the Reviewer reported no findings, but bind breaks property target 6 on every invalid input tried (mutation-a1.edn :contract-probe): an unknown operator throws expr/binary's ex-info with no :sandbox/error, nil overflows the stack, and malformed :vars the Bindings resolve come back as valid literals. The implementation validates its OUTPUT, not its input. NOT the Coder's fault alone: target 6 lived only in :property-targets, which coder-packet does not carry (NOTES row 4) - the decisions put in the title to work around that missed this one. So the feedback states the requirement outright. Sent as :triage, a source added for this, rather than misattributed to the Reviewer. PREDICTION: the Coder moves check! to the input and keeps the rest.", :event/at-ms 324437} {:role :coder, :feedback [#:feedback{:from :triage, :text "The contract also requires this, and your packet did not carry it: bind throws the ex-info that sandbox.shapes/check! throws (ex-data :sandbox/error :invalid-expr) when e does not satisfy Expr. Your bind validates the tree it returns, not the e it was given. Against your file: an operator node with :expr/op :pow throws a different ex-info with no :sandbox/error; (bind nil b) overflows the stack; and {:expr/op :var :expr/name \"\"}, or a :var with an extra key, is returned as a literal when the Bindings resolve its name. Every one of those must throw check!'s ex-info. Keep the rest of the behaviour as it is."}], :tool-calls 8, :event/kind :dispatch, :capped? false, :event/at-ms 362789, :status :done, :timing {:ms-completion 37478, :ms-provenance 0, :ms-provenance-wait 0, :ms-tools 717, :reasoning-tokens nil, :completions 7}, :notes [], :iterations 7, :files ["src/sandbox/bind.clj"], :service-tier nil, :provider nil, :attempt 2, :event/step :coder-r1, :stdout "All four previously-failing cases from the triage now throw